# MedSight AI — Model Choice & Dataset Guide

---

## 1. The Dataset Situation

### What Data Do We Need?

For **anomaly detection** in chest X-rays, the key insight is:
> You only train on **NORMAL** images. The model learns what "healthy" looks like. Anything that looks different from normal = anomaly.

This is called **unsupervised anomaly detection** — you don't need radiologists to label every disease. You just need a big set of normal scans.

### Available Datasets

| Dataset | Images | Size | License | Normal subset |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **NIH ChestX-ray14** | 112,120 | ~42GB (images) | Free, public | ~60,000 "No Finding" images |
| **CheXpert (Stanford)** | 224,316 | ~40GB | Free for research | Register at cheXpert.stanford.edu |
| **MIMIC-CXR** | 377,110 | ~100GB | Requires PhysioNet account | Credentialing takes 1–2 weeks |
| **VinDr-CXR** | 18,000 | ~7GB | Free (Kaggle) | Good quality, Vietnamese population |
| **PadChest** | 160,000 | ~100GB | Free for research | Spanish dataset |

### ✅ Recommended: NIH ChestX-ray14 "No Finding" Subset

- **Download link**: https://nihcc.app.box.com/v/ChestXray-NIHCC
- You only need the images labeled `No Finding` in `Data_Entry_2017.csv`
- That gives you ~26,000 frontal chest X-rays of healthy patients
- Size: ~6GB (just the normal subset)
- Completely free, no registration

> **For BioBERT NER training** (separate task):
> - NCBI Disease Corpus: ~793 PubMed abstracts, annotated with disease names — ~1MB, free
> - BC5CDR: 1,500 PubMed articles with chemical+disease NER — free

---

## 2. Why DINOv2? — And Why It May Be Wrong For You

### What DINOv2 Does in This Project

The original plan uses DINOv2 as a **frozen feature extractor**:
```
Image → DINOv2 (frozen, ~400MB) → 384-dim feature vector → Small Autoencoder Head → Reconstruction Error
```

DINOv2 itself is NOT trained — only the small projection head is trained. DINOv2 just extracts rich visual features.

### Why Someone Would Choose DINOv2
- Fantastic feature quality — trained on 142M images via self-supervised learning
- Works with **very few normal training images** (even 500 images can work)
- No need to design a CNN architecture

### Why DINOv2 is WRONG for your production use case

| Problem | Reality |
|---|---|
| **Model size** | 400MB just for the feature extractor. Plus your head. Total ~450MB |
| **Inference speed** | ViT models are slow on CPU. ~5-15 seconds per image on CPU server |
| **Memory** | 400MB VRAM minimum to run efficiently |
| **Dependency cost** | Requires `transformers`, `timm`, PyTorch — heavy stack |
| **ONNX export** | Possible but complex — the attention mechanism adds overhead |
| **Cost on cloud** | GPU servers cost $50–200/month. CPU inference is too slow with DINOv2 |

---

## 3. Better Architecture: Custom ConvAE

### What is a Convolutional Autoencoder (ConvAE)?

```
Input Image (1×224×224)
      ↓
  [ENCODER] — Convolutional layers that compress
      ↓
  [BOTTLENECK] — e.g., 16×14×14 = 3,136 values (compressed representation)
      ↓
  [DECODER] — Transposed convolutions that reconstruct
      ↓
Reconstructed Image (1×224×224)
      ↓
MSE Loss vs. Original = Reconstruction Error
      ↓
High Error → ANOMALY | Low Error → NORMAL
```

**The logic:** The model learns to perfectly reconstruct normal chest X-rays. When you feed an abnormal image, it can't reconstruct it well → high MSE → anomaly detected.

### ConvAE vs DINOv2 Head: Comparison

| | **ConvAE (Custom)** | **DINOv2 + Head** |
|---|---|---|
| Model size | **~2–8MB** | ~450MB |
| Inference speed (CPU) | **0.1–0.5 sec** | 5–15 sec |
| ONNX export | **Easy** | Complex |
| Training time | ~2 hrs (GPU) | ~2 hrs (GPU) |
| Training data needed | 10,000+ images | 500+ images |
| Feature quality | Good (domain-specific) | Excellent (general) |
| Deployable on cheap CPU server | **Yes** | No (too slow) |
| Fully yours (no HuggingFace dep) | **Yes** | No |

### ConvAE Architecture for Chest X-rays

```python
class ChestConvAE(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        # ENCODER: 1×224×224 → 64×7×7
        self.encoder = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Conv2d(1, 32, 4, stride=2, padding=1),   # 32×112×112
            nn.BatchNorm2d(32), nn.LeakyReLU(0.2),
            nn.Conv2d(32, 64, 4, stride=2, padding=1),  # 64×56×56
            nn.BatchNorm2d(64), nn.LeakyReLU(0.2),
            nn.Conv2d(64, 128, 4, stride=2, padding=1), # 128×28×28
            nn.BatchNorm2d(128), nn.LeakyReLU(0.2),
            nn.Conv2d(128, 128, 4, stride=2, padding=1),# 128×14×14
            nn.BatchNorm2d(128), nn.LeakyReLU(0.2),
            nn.Conv2d(128, 64, 4, stride=2, padding=1), # 64×7×7 (bottleneck)
            nn.BatchNorm2d(64), nn.LeakyReLU(0.2),
        )
        # DECODER: 64×7×7 → 1×224×224
        self.decoder = nn.Sequential(
            nn.ConvTranspose2d(64, 128, 4, stride=2, padding=1),  # 128×14×14
            nn.BatchNorm2d(128), nn.ReLU(),
            nn.ConvTranspose2d(128, 128, 4, stride=2, padding=1), # 128×28×28
            nn.BatchNorm2d(128), nn.ReLU(),
            nn.ConvTranspose2d(128, 64, 4, stride=2, padding=1),  # 64×56×56
            nn.BatchNorm2d(64), nn.ReLU(),
            nn.ConvTranspose2d(64, 32, 4, stride=2, padding=1),   # 32×112×112
            nn.BatchNorm2d(32), nn.ReLU(),
            nn.ConvTranspose2d(32, 1, 4, stride=2, padding=1),    # 1×224×224
            nn.Sigmoid()  # Output: pixel values 0-1
        )
    
    def forward(self, x):
        return self.decoder(self.encoder(x))
```

**Bottleneck:** 64×7×7 = 3,136 values from a 224×224 image = **50:1 compression ratio**

**Total parameters:** ~2.3M — trains in 2 hours on a decent GPU, exports to ONNX at ~8MB.

---

## 4. Production / Web Deployment Reality

### What "Run on Web" Means

There are two very different things:

**Option A: Browser-side inference (WebAssembly / TensorFlow.js / ONNX Runtime Web)**
- Model runs IN the user's browser — no server needed for inference
- Works for tiny models (<25MB)
- Our ConvAE (8MB) could work here with ONNX.js
- BioBERT is 440MB — NOT possible in browser
- BioGPT is 700MB — NOT possible in browser

**Option B: Server-side inference (FastAPI + uvicorn, deployed on cloud)**
- User sends image → server runs model → returns result
- This is what MedSight currently is designed to do
- Works for ALL model sizes
- Needs a cloud server (Render, Railway, Fly.io, AWS, etc.)

### For MedSight AI: Server-side is the right choice

Medical imaging analysis should always be server-side because:
1. Models are too large for in-browser (BioGPT, BioBERT)
2. Medical data privacy — you control what happens on your server
3. Consistent performance — not dependent on user's device

### Production Server Options (Realistic for Your Budget)

| Platform | GPU? | Price/month | Suitable? |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Render.com** | No GPU | $7–25 | ✅ Yes — with ConvAE + ONNX |
| **Railway.app** | No GPU | $5–20 | ✅ Yes — with ConvAE + ONNX |
| **Fly.io** | No GPU | $3–30 | ✅ Yes — with ConvAE + ONNX |
| **Hugging Face Spaces** | GPU (shared) | Free–$25 | ✅ Good for demo |
| **AWS EC2 t3.medium** | No GPU | ~$30 | ✅ Stable, scalable |
| **Vast.ai** | GPU | $0.1–0.5/hr | ✅ For training only |

**With ConvAE (~8MB ONNX), you can run on the cheapest CPU server ($7/month) and still get inference in 0.3 seconds.**  
**With DINOv2 (~450MB PyTorch), you need a GPU server ($50–200/month) to get acceptable speed.**

